{
 "_note": "The DSX Scene ANIMATION corpus (dsx-scene.md P5). THE OVERRIDE LAW: an animation produces a value that OVERRIDES the authored/bound base value of ONE property while active; when it ends, the property returns to base (or holds, per fill=\"hold\"). THE TARGET SET is closed: position · rotation · scale (vec3) · color (LINEAR-RGB triple) · intensity · fov (scalars); opacity is a NAMED ABSENCE (no material opacity channel). EASING IS PINNED MATH — the constants and formulas are in the corpus README: linear; ease/ease-in/ease-out/ease-in-out as the CSS cubic-bezier constants solved by exactly 60 bisection iterations; spring(stiffness,damping) as the analytic mass-1 damped spring on the REAL clock (duration is ignored; the clip completes at the settle time T = ln(1000)/(ωn·(ζ − √max(0, ζ²−1))) where progress clamps to exactly 1). Interpolation is plain componentwise lerp on the value plane (colors linearized first). value(t) is a PURE function of the spec + start state — `tweens` cases sample sceneTweenValue at pinned times (ms since clip start; `from` defaults to the base at start), `gated` cases pin the `when` law (falsy ⇒ the property shows base; each falsy→truthy edge RESTARTS the clock; on:done does not fire on a gate stop), and `transitions` cases pin the CSS-model retarget fold (a base change starts a glide FROM THE CURRENT RENDERED VALUE toward the new base; interrupt = start from where you are, never snap, never queue; the entry's delay HOLDS the pre-change value). Loop law: delay applies ONCE; loop=true cycles (u = 0 at the exact wrap instant); loop=N completes after N cycles with on:done ONCE; pingpong samples the easing at (clip − u) on odd cycles — the same curve traversed backwards. All floats 6 decimals, tolerance 1.5e-6; every number from an independent scratch implementation. Runners: TS (per-PR). Kotlin/Swift are the NAMED P5 follow-up (dsx-scene.md P5 row).",
